`VectorStore.search()` (`src/agents/tier1/memory-agent.ts:170`) and `HistoricalStore.search()` (`src/agents/tier2/historical-memory-agent.ts:216`) both do a full `for` loop over **every stored embedding**, computing cosine similarity against each one, on every single search call — despite docstrings claiming "HNSW-powered similarity search." If `OrchestratorAgent` or any tier-2+ agent calls `historicalMemory.search()` once per campaign in a loop (a very likely usage pattern given `analyze_trends`/`find_similar` are called per-campaign), that's O(campaigns × storedPatterns), which is the exact same shape as an N+1 problem.

```ts
// src/agents/tier2/historical-memory-agent.ts — current
async search(query: number[], limit = 10, minScore = 0.5) {
  const results = [];
  for (const [id, embedding] of this.embeddings) {   // full scan, every call
    const score = this.cosineSimilarity(query, embedding);
    ...
  }
}
```

Fix: either batch the queries (score once against the full set, return multiple results) instead of calling `.search()` per item in a loop, or swap in a real ANN index (there's a `agentdb`/HNSW toolset available in this environment already — worth actually wiring it in rather than hand-rolling a mock). At minimum, cap `this.embeddings.size` and pre-filter by `type`/`platform` before the similarity loop.

Also worth noting: `cosineSimilarity` is duplicated verbatim in both files (`memory-agent.ts:247`, `historical-memory-agent.ts:303`) — extract to one shared `src/core/vector-math.ts`.

**a) `StateManager.tasks` grows forever** — `src/core/state-manager.ts`. `addTask`/`updateTask` both `.set()` into `state.tasks`, but grep shows **no `delete` anywhere in the file**. Every task ever submitted by any agent stays in memory for the process lifetime, and `getTasksByStatus`/`getQueuedTaskCount` (`state-manager.ts:221,402`) re-scan the entire growing map on every call.

```ts
// add a completion-based eviction, e.g. in updateTask:
updateTask(taskId: string, updates: Partial<Task>): void {
  const task = this.state.tasks.get(taskId);
  if (!task) return;
  const updated = { ...task, ...updates };
  this.state.tasks.set(taskId, updated);
  if (updated.status === 'completed' || updated.status === 'failed') {
    // move to a bounded ring buffer / TTL cache instead of leaving in the live map
    this.scheduleTaskEviction(taskId, this.config.taskRetentionMs ?? 60_000);
  }
}
```

**b) `HistoricalStore` (tier2) has zero cleanup** — unlike `VectorStore` in `memory-agent.ts`, which has `cleanupMemory()` wired to a `setInterval` in `onInitialize`/`onShutdown` (`memory-agent.ts:539-556`, correctly cleared), `HistoricalStore.patterns`/`.embeddings` in `historical-memory-agent.ts` have **no cap, no TTL, no periodic cleanup at all** (confirmed by grep — no `cleanup`/`maxSize`/eviction logic in that file). It only grows via `store()`.

**c) `BaseAgent.requestAgent` — unbounded polling with no timeout** (`src/agents/base-agent.ts:365-410`):

```ts
return new Promise((resolve) => {
  const checkInterval = setInterval(() => {
    const updatedTask = this.stateManager.getTask(task.id);
    if (updatedTask?.status === 'completed' || updatedTask?.status === 'failed') {
      clearInterval(checkInterval);
      resolve(...);
    }
  }, 100);
});
```
If the target agent never marks the task completed/failed (crash, dropped event, unknown agent id), this `setInterval` polls every 100ms **forever** — the interval, the closure, and the corresponding entry in the never-cleaned `state.tasks` map (see 2a) all leak permanently, and the returned promise never resolves/rejects, so callers hang too. Needs a timeout that clears the interval and rejects/resolves with a failure:

```ts
return new Promise((resolve) => {
  const checkInterval = setInterval(() => { ... }, 100);
  const timeout = setTimeout(() => {
    clearInterval(checkInterval);
    resolve({ success: false, error: 'Task timed out', metrics: { processingTime: 0, memoryUsed: 0 } });
  }, this.config.timeoutMs);
  // clear timeout in the completion branch too
});
```
Better long-term fix: replace polling entirely with an event-bus subscription (`this.eventBus.subscribeOnce('task.completed', ...)`) — the codebase already has this exact pattern in `event-bus.ts:295-299` (`subscribeOnce`), it's just not used here.

**`recommendHashtags` rescoring on every call** (`src/campaigns/hashtag-analyzer.ts`): `scoreHashtag()` is called for every entry in the static `HASHTAG_DB` on every single call to `recommendHashtags`, even though `HASHTAG_DB` is a hardcoded constant and `niche`/`platform` are the only variables — the scores are trivially memoizable per `(niche, platform)` pair:

```ts
const scoreCache = new Map<string, HashtagScore[]>();

function getScoredHashtags(niche: string, platform: SocialPlatform): HashtagScore[] {
  const key = `${platform}:${niche}`;
  let cached = scoreCache.get(key);
  if (!cached) {
    cached = Object.keys(HASHTAG_DB)
      .map(tag => scoreHashtag(tag, niche, platform))
      .sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score);
    scoreCache.set(key, cached);
  }
  return cached;
}
```

**`MonteCarloEngine.simulate`** (`src/agents/tier2/simulation-agent.ts`) reruns 10,000 iterations per variation with no memoization — if `SimulationAgent` is asked to re-simulate the same campaign/scenario repeatedly (e.g. from `CounterfactualAgent` and `IncrementalityAuditorAgent`, both of which depend on `simulation`), an LRU cache keyed on `(baseMetrics, variation.changes)` would avoid redundant iteration work. The existing `src/performance/cache.ts` `LRUCache` class is already built for exactly this and isn't used anywhere in the agent layer — wire it in:

```ts
private cache = new LRUCache<SimulationResult[]>({ maxSize: 500, defaultTtlMs: 60_000 });

async simulate(baseMetrics, variations, iterations = 10000) {
  const key = JSON.stringify({ baseMetrics, variations });
  const cached = this.cache.get(key);
  if (cached) return cached;
  const results = /* existing loop */;
  this.cache.set(key, results);
  return results;
}
```

**`analyzePlatform` / `generateCampaignReport`** (`src/campaigns/analytics.ts`): for each platform, `analyzePlatform` does `posts.filter(p => p.platform === platform)`, so `generateCampaignReport` scans the full `posts` array once per platform (O(platforms × posts)) instead of grouping once:

```ts
// instead of calling analyzePlatform(posts, p, period) per platform (each re-filtering `posts`):
const postsByPlatform = groupBy(posts, p => p.platform); // single pass
const platforms = campaign.platforms.map(p =>
  analyzePlatformFromGrouped(postsByPlatform[p] ?? [], p, period)
);
```
Same fix applies to the repeated `groupBy(platformPosts, ...)` calls inside `analyzePlatform` for time-slot/day/format — three separate passes over `platformPosts` that could be combined into one reduce.

**`OrchestratorAgent`/`RiskDetectionAgent` history arrays** — these are fine (both correctly capped at 1000 with `.slice(-1000)`, confirmed in `orchestrator-agent.ts:203-205` and `risk-detection-agent.ts:637-639`), so no action needed there — flagging only to say I checked and they're *not* leaks, unlike `HistoricalStore`.

